---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { images } from '../data/assets';
const routes=[
  {title:'Multi-Day Trekking',copy:'Point-to-point or lodge-supported journeys where the route itself is the experience.',image:images.trekking,tags:['Trails','Multi-day']},
  {title:'Mountain & Peaks',copy:'Mountain travel shaped around realistic objectives, acclimatization, pacing, and support.',image:images.fuji,tags:['Mountains','Objectives']},
  {title:'Active Cultural Travel',copy:'Move through a region on foot or by activity while keeping culture, food, and local context central.',image:images.marrakech,tags:['Culture','Movement']}
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Trekking & Active Group Travel | Talon Expeditions" description="Supported trekking, hiking, mountain and active group travel for clubs, communities and private groups worldwide." image={images.trekking}>
  <section class="page-hero" style={`background-image:url('${images.trekking}')`}><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Trekking & Active Travel</p><h1 class="section-title">Move through the place instead of only looking at it.</h1><p>Supported hiking, trekking, mountain, and active journeys where pacing, preparation, and logistics are designed around the group.</p></div></section>

  <section class="specialty-intro"><div class="shell"><div class="specialty-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Trip styles</p><h2 class="section-title">Active does not have to mean extreme.</h2></div><p class="section-copy">The useful question is whether the route, challenge, accommodation, support, and pace fit the people who are actually going.</p></div><div class="destination-grid">{routes.map((item)=><a class="destination-card" href="/contact/" style={`background-image:url('${item.image}')`}><div class="destination-content"><span class="tagline">Active journey</span><h3>{item.title}</h3><p>{item.copy}</p><div class="mini-tags">{item.tags.map((tag)=><span>{tag}</span>)}</div></div></a>)}</div></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="detail-gallery"><div class="gallery-main" style={`background-image:url('${images.lake}')`}></div><div style={`background-image:url('${images.road}')`}></div><div style={`background-image:url('${images.backpacker}')`}></div></div><div class="detail-body"><aside><div class="trip-facts"><h3>Build around the route and the people.</h3><div class="fact"><strong>Intensity</strong><span>Beginner-friendly through demanding objectives.</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Accommodation</strong><span>Comfort-forward, lodge-based, camping, or mixed formats.</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Support</strong><span>Local guides, transfers, luggage support, logistics, and preparation as appropriate.</span></div></div><div class="day-grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;margin-top:12px"><div class="day-card"><span class="day-label">Before departure</span><h3>Readiness matters.</h3><p>Fitness expectations, footwear, equipment, weather, terrain, and any skill requirements should be clear long before the airport.</p></div></div></aside><div class="overview-panel"><p class="eyebrow">What a strong active itinerary includes</p><h2>The route, recovery, logistics, and support all have to agree with each other.</h2><p>A beautiful trail is not enough. Active travel works when the operational plan respects what a group can do repeatedly for several days and what they still want to enjoy after the walking stops.</p><div class="check-grid"><span>Daily distance and elevation that make sense</span><span>Realistic transfers and start times</span><span>Weather and seasonality considered</span><span>Optionality where mixed abilities need it</span><span>Equipment and packing expectations</span><span>Recovery, food, and accommodation matched to effort</span></div><h3>Example journey rhythm</h3><div class="day-grid"><div class="day-card"><span class="day-label">Day type 01</span><h3>Build In</h3><p>Arrival, orientation, equipment check, shorter movement, and an honest read on the group.</p></div><div class="day-card"><span class="day-label">Day type 02</span><h3>Go Long</h3><p>The main trekking days where distance, terrain, logistics, and support work as one system.</p></div><div class="day-card"><span class="day-label">Day type 03</span><h3>Recover & Discover</h3><p>Lower-intensity movement, culture, food, or rest so the trip remains enjoyable rather than simply survivable.</p></div></div></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="feature-band section" style={`background-image:url('${images.fuji}')`}><div class="shell"><div class="band-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Preparation is part of the experience</p><h2 class="section-title">A stronger group at departure has a better trip on the ground.</h2><p>For more demanding travel, Talon can make readiness expectations visible early so travelers understand the fitness, equipment, skills, and conditions they are signing up for.</p><a class="button" href="/contact/">Discuss an Active Trip</a></div></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Why plan it this way</p><h2 class="section-title">Four things that keep active travel enjoyable.</h2><div class="why-grid"><div class="why-item"><span class="number">01</span><h3>Right-sized challenge</h3><p>Ambitious enough to matter without designing the trip for an imaginary athlete.</p></div><div class="why-item"><span class="number">02</span><h3>Local competence</h3><p>Operators and guides appropriate to terrain, activity, season, and objective.</p></div><div class="why-item"><span class="number">03</span><h3>Operational support</h3><p>The transport, lodging, meals, luggage, timing, and contingency pieces that make the route possible.</p></div><div class="why-item"><span class="number">04</span><h3>Traveler readiness</h3><p>Clear preparation reduces avoidable friction before and during the journey.</p></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="specialty-cta"><div class="shell"><h2>Tell us the challenge your group is ready for.</h2><a class="button button-secondary" href="/contact/">Plan an Active Journey</a></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
